<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE refentry PUBLIC "-//OASIS//DTD DocBook XML V4.5//EN"
  "http://www.oasis-open.org/docbook/xml/4.5/docbookx.dtd">

<refentry id="ndm_setupgroups">

<refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>ndm_setupgroups</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>1</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo class="source">ndmanager-plugins</refmiscinfo>
  <refmiscinfo class="manual">NeuroSuite-3 Tools</refmiscinfo>
</refmeta>

<refnamediv>
  <refname>ndm_setupgroups</refname>
  <refpurpose>
    Build anatomical and spike-detection channel groups from probe library entries
  </refpurpose>
</refnamediv>

<refsynopsisdiv>
  <cmdsynopsis>
    <command>ndm_setupgroups</command>
    <arg choice="opt"><replaceable>parameter-file</replaceable></arg>
  </cmdsynopsis>
</refsynopsisdiv>

<refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>
  <para>
    <command>ndm_setupgroups</command> reads the <literal>probes:</literal>
    section of the session YAML, loads each referenced
    <filename>.probe</filename> configuration file from the probe library, and
    writes <literal>anatomicalDescription.channelGroups</literal> and
    <literal>spikeDetection.channelGroups</literal> into the session YAML
    in-place.
  </para>
  <para>
    One anatomical group and one corresponding spike-detection group are created
    per shank.  The two tables are always in sync: the spike-detection groups
    mirror the anatomical groups with the same channel lists, supplemented by
    the waveform-window parameters <parameter>nSamples</parameter>,
    <parameter>peakSampleIndex</parameter>, and <parameter>nFeatures</parameter>.
  </para>
  <para>
    Probe and shank metadata (<literal>probeId</literal>,
    <literal>shankIndex</literal>) are written into each anatomical group entry
    so that <citerefentry><refentrytitle>ndm_estimatedrift</refentrytitle>
    <manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry> can reconstruct the spatial geometry
    of each shank without re-reading the probe library.  The
    <literal>probes[].anatomicalGroups</literal> field is updated to reflect the
    newly assigned group IDs.
  </para>
  <para>
    <command>ndm_setupgroups</command> must be run <emphasis>before</emphasis>
    any spike-detection step
    (<citerefentry><refentrytitle>ndm_detectspikes</refentrytitle>
    <manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>,
    <citerefentry><refentrytitle>ndm_extractspikes</refentrytitle>
    <manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>,
    <citerefentry><refentrytitle>ndm_pca</refentrytitle>
    <manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>,
    <citerefentry><refentrytitle>ndm_klustakwik</refentrytitle>
    <manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>).
  </para>
</refsect1>

<refsect1>
  <title>Channel Assignment</title>
  <para>
    When <literal>channelMap.map</literal> is <literal>null</literal> (the
    default for all NeuroNexus probe files), channels are assigned sequentially:
  </para>
  <blockquote>
    <para>
      shank <replaceable>i</replaceable> →
      [<replaceable>offset</replaceable> + <replaceable>i</replaceable>·<replaceable>n</replaceable>,
       …,
       <replaceable>offset</replaceable> + (<replaceable>i</replaceable>+1)·<replaceable>n</replaceable> − 1]
    </para>
  </blockquote>
  <para>
    where <replaceable>offset</replaceable> = <literal>probes[].channelOffset</literal>
    and <replaceable>n</replaceable> = <literal>probeFile.sites.count_per_shank</literal>.
    <literal>channelOffset</literal> is the probe's first channel, absolute
    (the value ndmanager writes, and the one Klusters and the drift tools
    read).  An entry without <literal>channelOffset</literal> follows the
    previous probe (that probe's offset plus its channel count); the offset
    used is written back to the entry.
  </para>
  <para>
    When <literal>channelMap.map</literal> is present it is used directly
    (flat or shank-major nested list), with <literal>channelOffset</literal>
    added to each entry.
  </para>
  <para>
    Channel ordering within each group follows the probe file order: top
    site first, tip site last, so channel numbers ascend with depth.  This
    ordering is required by <command>ndm_extractspikes_sdiff</command>
    (spatial neighbours adjacent); the drift and localisation tools look
    positions up by channel number and accept any order.  Site
    <replaceable>j</replaceable> of the probe file is channel
    channelOffset + <replaceable>j</replaceable> (or channelOffset +
    channelMap.map[<replaceable>j</replaceable>]); sites.count_per_shank is
    one number or a list with one entry per shank.
  </para>
</refsect1>

<refsect1>
  <title>Options</title>
  <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
      <term><parameter>nSamples</parameter></term>
      <listitem>
        <para>
          Total waveform window width in samples written to each spike-detection
          group.  At 32 kHz a 1.625 ms window requires 52 samples; at 20 kHz
          use 32.  Default: <literal>52</literal>.
        </para>
      </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
      <term><parameter>peakSampleIndex</parameter></term>
      <listitem>
        <para>
          Zero-based index of the expected waveform peak within the
          <parameter>nSamples</parameter> window.  Default:
          <literal>26</literal> (midpoint of a 52-sample window).
        </para>
      </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
      <term><parameter>nFeatures</parameter></term>
      <listitem>
        <para>
          Number of PCA components per channel used by
          <command>ndm_pca</command> and <command>ndm_klustakwik</command>.
          Default: <literal>3</literal>.
        </para>
      </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
      <term><parameter>probeLibrary</parameter></term>
      <listitem>
        <para>
          An extra probe library directory.  A relative
          <literal>probeFile</literal> is looked up in these directories,
          first match wins (the order ndmanager uses):
          the session directory (where ndmanager keeps
          <filename>SESSION.probe.ID.probe</filename>),
          <filename>SESSION_DIR/probes/</filename>,
          <parameter>probeLibrary</parameter>,
          the session's top-level <literal>probeLibraryPath</literal>,
          <envar>NEUROSUITE_PROBE_PATH</envar> and the legacy
          <envar>NS3_PROBE_LIBRARY_PATH</envar> (colon-separated),
          then <filename>neurosuite/probes/</filename> under
          <envar>XDG_DATA_HOME</envar> (<filename>~/.local/share</filename>)
          and each of <envar>XDG_DATA_DIRS</envar>
          (<filename>/usr/local/share</filename>,
          <filename>/usr/share</filename>).  An absolute
          <literal>probeFile</literal> is used as is.
        </para>
      </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
      <term><parameter>overwrite</parameter></term>
      <listitem>
        <para>
          If <literal>false</literal> (default), the plugin aborts when
          <literal>anatomicalDescription</literal> or
          <literal>spikeDetection</literal> groups already exist in the session
          YAML.  Set to <literal>true</literal> to replace them.
        </para>
      </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
</refsect1>

<refsect1>
  <title>Session YAML Requirements</title>
  <para>
    The session YAML must contain a <literal>probes:</literal> sequence with
    at least one entry providing:
  </para>
  <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        <literal>probeFile</literal> — path relative to the session directory
        or a probe library (e.g. <filename>neuronexus/Buzsaki64.probe</filename>,
        or ndmanager's <filename>SESSION.probe.0.probe</filename>), or absolute.
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        <literal>channelOffset</literal> — first channel of this probe,
        absolute and 0-based.  When absent the probe follows the previous one
        (the first probe starts at 0).
      </para>
    </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
</refsect1>

<refsect1>
  <title>Example</title>
  <para>
    Session YAML fragment before running <command>ndm_setupgroups</command>:
  </para>
  <programlisting>
probes:
  - probeId: 0
    probeFile: neuronexus/Buzsaki64.probe
    label: "left CA1"
    channelOffset: 0
  - probeId: 1
    probeFile: neuronexus/Buzsaki64.probe
    label: "right CA1"
    channelOffset: 64
  </programlisting>
  <para>
    After running the plugin (Buzsaki64: 8 shanks × 8 channels):
  </para>
  <programlisting>
probes:
  - probeId: 0
    probeFile: neuronexus/Buzsaki64.probe
    label: "left CA1"
    channelOffset: 0
    anatomicalGroups: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
  - probeId: 1
    probeFile: neuronexus/Buzsaki64.probe
    label: "right CA1"
    channelOffset: 64
    anatomicalGroups: [9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16]

anatomicalDescription:
  channelGroups:
    - channels: [{id: 0, skip: 0}, …, {id: 7, skip: 0}]
      probeId: 0
      shankIndex: 0
    …

spikeDetection:
  channelGroups:
    - channels: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
      nSamples: 52
      peakSampleIndex: 26
      nFeatures: 3
    …
  </programlisting>
</refsect1>

